    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.add_havel_hakimi_graph - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.add_havel_hakimi_graph.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_havel_hakimi_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Add a graph with a given degree sequence, built deterministically by the\n    Havel-Hakimi\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_havel_hakimi_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Add a graph with a given degree sequence, built deterministically by the\n    Havel-Hakimi\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.add_havel_hakimi_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Add a graph with a given degree sequence, built deterministically by the\n  Havel-Hakimi\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nAdd a graph with a given degree sequence, built deterministically by the Havel-Hakimi\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L13599-L13752){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.add_havel_hakimi_graph(\n    degree_sequence,\n    type=None,\n    label=True,\n    rel=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\nalgorithm.\n\n![Havel-Hakimi graph](../assets/graphs/add-havel-hakimi-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Three Havel-Hakimi graphs on five nodes, each node's requested degree in a pill: 3, 2, 2, 2, 1; 3, 3, 2, 2, 2; and 4, 3, 3, 2, 2. The construction is deterministic, joining the node with the most degree left to the next-highest nodes, and every node ends with exactly the degree in its pill, using 5, 6, and 7 edges.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nCreates one node per entry of `degree_sequence`, and node `i` gets exactly\n`degree_sequence[i]` neighbors, with no self-loops or parallel edges. The Havel-Hakimi\nalgorithm repeatedly takes the node with the largest remaining degree `d` and joins it\nto the `d` nodes with the next-largest remaining degrees. It succeeds exactly when the\nsequence is graphical (some simple graph has these degrees), which is checked first\nwith the Erdős-Gallai test.\n\nThere is no randomness: the same sequence always gives the same graph. Because\nhigh-degree nodes are joined first, they tend to link to each other, so the result is\nstrongly assortative. For a random graph with the same degrees, see\n`add_random_degree_sequence_graph()` (simple) or `configuration_model()` (with\nself-loops and parallel edges). The process follows NetworkX's `havel_hakimi_graph()`\nand gives the same edges. NetworkX numbers only the nodes of nonzero degree, though, so\nwhen the sequence contains zeros its node `i` may not have degree `degree_sequence[i]`;\nhere it always does.\n\nIn a directed graph every edge points from the earlier-created node to the later one.\nThe graph is appended without touching or connecting to existing nodes, and the whole\nconstruction is recorded as a single step.\n\nHavel-Hakimi graphs are useful for:\n\n- checking that a degree sequence can be realized, and getting one realization\n- a deterministic, maximally assortative starting point for degree-preserving\n  rewiring (see `double_edge_swap()`)\n- test graphs with exactly prescribed degrees\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[degree_sequence]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Degree of each new node, as a list of non-negative integers. The sum must be even.\n\n<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),\n    no `type` attribute is set.\n\n<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; a label\n    that is already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, the\n    nodes are left unlabelled.\n\n<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),\n    no `rel` attribute is set.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `degree_sequence=` contains a negative entry or is not graphical.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~add_random_degree_sequence_graph`) \n\n:   Draw a random simple graph with the same degrees.\n\n[](`~configuration_model`) \n\n:   Pair edge ends at random, allowing loops and repeated edges.\n\n[](`~add_directed_havel_hakimi_graph`) \n\n:   Build a directed graph from in- and out-degrees.\n\n[](`~add_bipartite_havel_hakimi_graph`) \n\n:   Build a bipartite graph from the degrees of each part.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nEach node gets the degree at its position in the sequence:\n\n::: {#b972dd0d .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_havel_hakimi_graph([3, 3, 2, 2, 1, 1])\n\ngraph.get_node_df()[\"degree\"].to_list()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[3, 3, 2, 2, 1, 1]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe Havel–Hakimi construction repeatedly joins the node with the most edges still to place\nto the next-highest nodes, so the high-degree nodes are joined to each other first:\n\n::: {#e7aecf56 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph.get_edges_str_repr()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n['1--2', '1--3', '1--4', '2--3', '2--4', '5--6']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nA sequence with no simple realization is rejected. Two nodes of degree three would\neach need three distinct neighbors among only three others, two of which have degree\none:\n\n::: {#cc1f9880 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntry:\n    ec.Graph().add_havel_hakimi_graph([3, 3, 1, 1])\nexcept ValueError as err:\n    print(err)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\ndegree_sequence is not graphical: no simple graph has these degrees\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Hubs together or spread out\n\nTwo graphs with the same eleven degrees, and the three nodes of degree `4` colored. On the\nleft, the Havel–Hakimi graph packs the hubs and the two nodes of degree `3` into one dense\ncluster, which leaves nodes `\"10\"` and `\"11\"` joined only to each other, and its degree\ncorrelation is strongly positive. On the right, `add_random_degree_sequence_graph()`\nspreads the hubs through a single connected graph, with no correlation:\n\n::: {#e80b2550 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndegrees = [4, 4, 4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1]\nspread = ec.Graph().add_random_degree_sequence_graph(degrees, seed=5)\npacked = ec.Graph().add_havel_hakimi_graph(degrees)\n\nfor g in (packed, spread):\n    rows = g.get_node_df().select(\"id\", \"degree\").rows()\n    g.set_node_attrs(\"hub\", {n: d == 4 for n, d in rows})\n\ndef mixing(g):\n    return round(g.degree_pearson_correlation_coefficient(), 2)\n\n[(g.is_connected(), mixing(g)) for g in (packed, spread)]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n[(False, 0.57), (True, 0.0)]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#c8ae8236 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npacked.plot(\n    color_by=\"hub\", label_by=\"label\",\n    width=320, height=320, legend=False, seed=1,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f5